00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:05.210 align:start position:0%
after days of being trapped in the  Himalayan mountains in Nepal these  foreign trekkers and local guides have  made it back to the capital since the  nationwide lockdown was brought in to  contain the coronavirus many had been  waiting to return to safety there was a  lot of anxiety but there was also a lot  of confusion we didn't know what was  going on thousands of tourists from  Europe Australia South Korea the US and  Canada have been stuck in trekking  routes across the country Nepal's  Tourism Board says rescue operations are  under way more flights are yet to come  as per our information and till now ten  flights we have landed here in Kathmandu  and under two packs people from  different nationalities there the German  and French embassies have been working  with Nepalese authorities to repatriate  hundreds of their citizens using  chartered planes so with this doctrine  the pandemic is coming to Nepal whether  you like it or not Germany has much  better health care system than a pool so  I think the risk in Germany is much  smaller than here a lot of French people  thought about staying in Nepal because  the situation was better but now we are  also thinking about our jobs and want to  be closer to our families at this time  but some travelers from the UK and US  are still waiting for information on  what will happen next their ambassadors  say they are working on solutions  there will be flights coming into  Kathmandu to go back to London and to  take you home those flights will not be  free they will come in on a commercial  basis but we are working very hard with  the airlines to try to make sure that  the prices are not inflated so many  Americans are in fact outside of  Kathmandu and currently under lockdown  it strikes me that the thing that makes  Nepal such an attractive destination the  ability to get far off the beaten path  is also complicating our logistical work  right  we are currently bringing American  citizens in to Kathmandu from all over  Nepal tens of thousands of visitors come  here every spring for trekking and  mountaineering but now expeditions  businesses and major markets have closed  transport has come to a standstill and  the Nepalese government has extended a  nationwide lockdown but many foreigners  stranded here say they just want to go  home  Rahila muhammad al jazeera
 

